ns:\n","    default_rates = df.groupby(column)[target_col].mean() * 100\n","\n","    plt.plot(\n","        default_rates.index,\n","        default_rates.values,\n","        marker=\"o\",\n","        label=column\n","    )\n","\n","# Chart formatting\n","plt.title(\"Previous Repayment Behaviour and Default Rate\")\n","plt.xlabel(\"Repayment Status\")\n","plt.ylabel(\"Default Rate (%)\")\n","plt.xticks(range(-2, 9))\n","plt.grid(True, alpha=0.3)\n","plt.legend(title=\"Repayment Period\")\n","plt.tight_layout()\n","plt.show()"],"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/","height":145},"id":"1n7PqH2ETgoT","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1795452469279,"user_tz":-60,"elapsed":2582,"user":{"displayName":"saving grace","userId":"00948253844733493042"}},"outputId":"7f79bdef-bb64-4d97-baf7-38deb90398e3"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":["<Figure size 1200x700 with 1 Axes>"],"image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["The line chart illustrates the relationship between previous repayment behaviour and the likelihood of default in the following month across six repayment periods. The chart generally shows higher default rates among customers with more serious repayment delays. For example, repayment status 2 recorded default rates above 50% across all six periods. Customers with status 3 also recorded consistently high default rates. In contrast, customers with status -1 generally had lower default rates.\n","\n","This suggests that previous repayment behaviour is an important indicator of potential future default. Financial institutions can therefore use repayment history to identify customers who may require closer monitoring and improved credit-risk assessment. However, categories with very small customer counts should be interpreted cautiously."],"metadata":{"id":"VD4BbweOT525"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":[" Question 3\n","How do credit limits differ between customers who default and those who do not?"],"metadata":{"id":"hltKW8XYgKVu"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["plt.figure(figsize=(8, 5))\n","\n","sns.boxplot(\n","    x=\"default payment next month\",\n","    y=\"LIMIT_BAL\",\n","    data=df\n",")\n","\n","plt.title(\"Credit Limitations uDistribution by Default Status\")\n","plt.xlabel(\"Default Status (0 = No Default, 1 = Default)\")\n","plt.ylabel(\"Credit Limit\")\n","plt.show()"],"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/","height":167},"id":"ezQ67Vr8gcai","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1795556796315,"user_tz":-60,"elapsed":378,"user":{"displayName":"saving grace","userId":"00948253844733493042"}},"outputId":"7683b2f6-56cd-4940-df7d-89dc3fb0830d"},"execution_count":8,"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":["<Figure size 800x500 with 1 Axes>"],"image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":[],"metadata":{"id":"mT6cYLygihFB"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Question 4: How do credit limits differ between customers who default and those who do not?\n","\n","The box plot compares the distribution of credit limits between customers who defaulted and those who did not. The results show that customers who did not default generally had higher median credit limits than customers who defaulted.\n","\n","The middle 50% of credit limits also appears higher among customers who did not default. However, both groups contain customers with high credit limits, indicating that having a high credit limit does not necessarily guarantee successful repayment.\n","\n","These findings suggest that credit limit may be associated with default behaviour, but it should not be used as the only factor in assessing credit risk. Financial institutions should also consider previous repayment behaviour, outstanding balances and credit utilisation when making lending decisions."],"metadata":{"id":"2w8hF6exihJG"}}]}